  "id": "u06-l02",
  "unit": "u06-bazaar",
  "title": "Taka & prices",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Prices are just **number + টাকা** (*taka*): **পঞ্চাশ টাকা** 'fifty taka', **একশ টাকা** 'a hundred taka'. With no 'is' in the present, **চা দশ টাকা** simply means 'Tea (is) ten taka'. The currency sign is **৳**, and in English you'll see *Tk 50* or *50 taka*.\n\nHundreds are built with **-শ** (*-sho*): **একশ** 100, **দুইশ** 200, **পাঁচশ** 500. A thousand is **হাজার**, so **এক হাজার টাকা** = ৳1,000.\n\nWatch the pairs that sound alike:\n\n- **পঞ্চাশ** 50 vs **পনেরো** 15 vs **পাঁচশ** 500\n- **দুইশ** 200 vs **দুইটা** 'two (things)'\n- **দশ টাকা** 'ten taka' vs **দশটা** 'ten o'clock / ten things'\n\nA taka is 100 **পয়সা** (*poysha*). You'll rarely handle poysha coins today, but the word survives in sayings about money. **শুধু** (*shudhu*) 'only, just' is handy: **শুধু পঞ্চাশ টাকা** 'only fifty taka'.\n\n**Culture:** the notes you'll handle most are ৳10, ৳20, ৳50, ৳100, ৳200, ৳500 and ৳1,000, plus ৳1, ৳2 and ৳5 coins. Small vendors and rickshaw drivers often can't break a ৳500 or ৳1,000 note, so keep small notes for the bazaar. Price tags may use Bengali digits (Unit 5): ৫০ = 50.\n\n**Listening tip:** numbers come fast, so don't be shy to ask again: **কত?**",
